?}: {}\", p, e))\n}\n\npub fn load_rust() -> Library {\n    let p = rust_lib_path();\n    unsafe { Library::new(&p) }\n        .unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"failed to load Rust lib at {:?}: {}\", p, e))\n}\n\n// stdout capture -------------------------------------------------------------\n//\n// Both implementations call libc::printf, which writes to the C runtime's\n// stdout (file descriptor 1). To capture, we:\n//   1. Flush stdout\n//   2. Redirect fd 1 to the write end of a pipe via dup2()\n//   3. Run the closure\n//   4. Flush again, restore fd 1, and read from the pipe\n//\n// This is single-threaded; tests must NOT run in parallel for this. We use a\n// global mutex.\n\nuse std::io::Read;\nuse std::os::unix::io::AsRawFd;\nuse std::sync::Mutex;\n\nstatic STDOUT_LOCK: Mutex<()> = Mutex::new(());\n\npub fn capture_stdout<F: FnOnce()>(f: F) -> Vec<u8> {\n    let _guard = STDOUT_LOCK.lock().unwrap();\n\n    unsafe {\n        // Flush whatever Rust or libc has buffered so we don't accidentally\n        // capture earlier output.\n        libc::fflush(std::ptr::null_mut());\n\n        let mut pipe_fds = [0i32; 2];\n        if libc::pipe(pipe_fds.as_mut_ptr()) != 0 {\n            panic!(\"pipe() failed\");\n        }\n        let read_fd = pipe_fds[0];\n        let write_fd = pipe_fds[1];\n\n        let saved = libc::dup(1);\n        if saved < 0 {\n            libc::close(read_fd);\n            libc::close(write_fd);\n            panic!(\"dup(1) failed\");\n        }\n        if libc::dup2(write_fd, 1) < 0 {\n            libc::close(read_fd);\n            libc::close(write_fd);\n            libc::close(saved);\n            panic!(\"dup2 failed\");\n        }\n        libc::close(write_fd);\n\n        let result = std::panic::catch_unwind(std::panic::AssertUnwindSafe(f));\n\n        libc::fflush(std::ptr::null_mut());\n        libc::dup2(saved, 1);\n        libc::close(saved);\n\n        // Drain the pipe.\n        let mut out = Vec::new();\n        let mut buf = [0u8; 4096];\n        let read_file = std::fs::File::from_raw_fd_owned(read_fd);\n        let _ = read_file.try_clone(); // no-op; just keep imports\n        // Use std::io directly through a raw fd wrapper.\n        let mut reader = std::os::unix::io::BorrowedFd::borrow_raw(read_fd);\n        let _ = &mut reader;\n        // Easier approach: read with libc::read directly.\n        loop {\n            let n = libc::read(read_fd, buf.as_mut_ptr() as *mut libc::c_void, buf.len());\n            if n <= 0 {\n                break;\n            }\n            out.extend_from_slice(&buf[..n as usize]);\n        }\n        libc::close(read_fd);\n\n        if let Err(p) = result {\n            std::panic::resume_unwind(p);\n        }\n        out\n    }\n}\n\n// Helper trait: load symbol or panic\npub fn sym<'a, T>(lib: &'a Library, name: &[u8]) -> Symbol<'a, T> {\n    unsafe { lib.get(name) }\n        .unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"symbol {} missing: {}\", String::from_utf8_lossy(name), e))\n}\n\n// Trait for use in trivial wrappers\ntrait _Unused {}\n\n// File I/O suppression for warnings about std::fs::File\nfn _silence_unused() {\n    let _ = std::fs::File::open(\"/dev/null\");\n}\n\n// Provide a trait extension for File::from_raw_fd_owned (older Rust may not).\ntrait FromRawFdOwned {\n    fn from_raw_fd_owned(fd: i32) -> Self;\n}\nimpl FromRawFdOwned for std::fs::File {\n    fn from_raw_fd_owned(fd: i32) -> Self {\n        // SAFETY: caller passes ownership of fd\n        use std::os::unix::io::FromRawFd;\n        unsafe { std::fs::File::from_raw_fd(fd) }\n    }\n}\n\n// Make a [c_char; N] buffer from a Rust &str (NUL-terminated, fixed length).\npub fn cstr_buf<const N: usize>(s: &str) -> [c_char; N] {\n    let mut buf = [0 as c_char; N];\n    let bytes = s.as_bytes();\n    let copy = bytes.len().min(N - 1);\n    for i in 0..copy {\n        buf[i] = bytes[i] as c_char;\n    }\n    buf\n}\n\n// Force-link a tiny use of AsRawFd to keep the import alive without warnings.\nfn 